        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Underground Railroad</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from the slave states north to free states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> (above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>), south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and by sea to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>; routes also led into Indian territories and western territories</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">earlier escape routes from the 16th century; a network of safe houses organising from the 1780s among Northern abolitionist societies; growth to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a> of 1863; escapes to Union lines during the Civil War, 1861 to 1865</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a>, a conductor who had escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; William Still, who kept records and was sometimes called the Father of the Underground Railroad; Levi Coffin, said to have aided over 2,000 fugitives; Charles Turner Torrey; Thomas Smallwood; Thomas Downing; Mary Meachum; Silvia Hector Webber; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a>, a critic of its publicity</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">estimates of those who escaped range from 100,000 by 1850 to 500,000 or more by the end of the Civil War; at least 30,000, and possibly more than 100,000, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>; the network faded as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a></span></div>
