    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Underground Railroad was neither literally underground nor a railroad: it had no headquarters or governing body, and consisted of meeting points, secret routes, transport and safe houses kept by sympathisers and passed on by word of mouth.</li><li>Free and enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> were the main actors in the network, and most escapes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> had no help from an abolitionist; routes ran north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, to Native American lands and by sea to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>.</li><li>The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 drove thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and historians debate how many escaped: estimates run from 100,000 by 1850 to 500,000 or more by the end of the Civil War.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first known use of the term in print, on 10 August 1842, was a joke by the African American abolitionist Thomas Smallwood, who was quoting a Baltimore constable's exasperation that fugitives must be travelling by &quot;steam balloon&quot; or &quot;underground rail-road&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

