    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1526</span><span class="rf-text">Enslaved Africans revolt at the Spanish colony of San Miguel de Gualdape, and historians suggest they escaped to Shakori Indigenous communities</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1738</span><span class="rf-text">The Spanish establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a> for free Blacks in the St. Augustine area of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1793</span><span class="rf-text">The original Fugitive Slave Act makes it easier to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and return them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and spurs abolitionists to help the enslaved</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1842</span><span class="rf-text">Thomas Smallwood uses the term underground rail-road in print for the first time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1850</span><span class="rf-text">The Compromise of 1850 brings a more stringent Fugitive Slave Law, and by December an estimated 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> have taken refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">General Benjamin Butler keeps three men who fled to Fort Monroe, calling them contraband of war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1863</span><span class="rf-text">President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> issues the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> outlaws <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> except as punishment for a crime</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Charles II of Spain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_de_Miranda_Carreno_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan Carreño de Miranda, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Enslaved people escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as early as the 16th century, and many of the escapes were unaided. In 1526 the enslaved Africans of the Spanish colony of San Miguel de Gualdape in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> revolted, and historians suggest they escaped to Shakori Indigenous communities. From 1689 enslaved Africans fled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina-lowcountry">South Carolina Lowcountry</a> to Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> proclaimed Florida a safe haven for escaped slaves from British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, they began escaping there by the hundreds from as far north as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, and the Spanish established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-mose">Fort Mose</a> for free Blacks in the St. Augustine area in 1738. A network of safe houses generally known as the Underground Railroad began to organise in the 1780s among abolitionist societies in the North.</p><p>Under the original Fugitive Slave Act of 1793, officials from free states were required to assist slaveholders or their agents who recaptured fugitives, although some state legislatures prohibited this. The law made it easier for slaveholders and slave catchers to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and return them to slavery, and in some cases allowed them to enslave free blacks. It also created an eagerness among abolitionists to help enslaved people, which resulted in the growth of anti-slavery societies and the Underground Railroad.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Underground Railroad sought to carry people escaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to the abolitionist Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The escapees sought primarily to reach free states or Canada, and many of those who rode the Railroad considered Canada their final destination. Other routes led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where slavery had been abolished, to islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> that were not part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, to western territories and to Indian territories.</p><p>Its members borrowed the language of the railway. The people who risked capture were called passengers and those who aided them conductors; resting places were stations or depots held by station masters, and stockholders gave money or supplies. Using biblical references, fugitives called Canada the Promised Land or Heaven and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>, the boundary between slave and free states, the River Jordan. The Big Dipper, whose bowl points to the North Star, was the drinking gourd.</p><p>Believing that slavery was "contrary to the ethics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>", many Christian congregations and clergy took part, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Religious Society of Friends</a> (Quakers), Congregationalists, Wesleyan Methodists and Reformed Presbyterians, as well as the anti-slavery branches of denominations that split over the issue, such as the Methodist Episcopal Church and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a>. Groups of agents worked in organisations known as vigilance committees.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first known use of the name in print was on 10 August 1842, when Thomas Smallwood, writing in the Albany abolitionist newspaper the Tocsin of Liberty, quoted the Baltimore constable John Zell on "steam balloon" or "underground rail-road". Abolitionists quickly adopted the joke as a name for the whole enterprise. The Railroad had no published guides, maps or pamphlets. Information passed by word of mouth and in coded messages, and people usually travelled at night, about 10 to 20 miles to each station.</p><p>The Compromise of 1850 included a more stringent Fugitive Slave Law. Suspected fugitives had no right to a jury trial and could not testify on their own behalf; between 1850 and 1860, 343 freedom seekers were taken before a commissioner and 332 were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. A few weeks after the law passed, Black populations in Northern cities declined as people left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Between mid-February and early March 1851 one hundred free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and fugitives fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, and abolitionists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> guided 1,200 people to Canada. By December 1850 an estimated 3,000 African Americans had taken refuge there.</p><p>During the Civil War large numbers of freedom seekers escaped to Union lines rather than to the North. In 1861 General Benjamin Butler kept three men who had fled to Fort Monroe as contraband of war. On 12 May 1862 Robert Smalls and sixteen other enslaved people sailed a Confederate ship out of Charleston Harbor to the Union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman.jpg" alt="Harriet Tubman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harriet_Tubman_(circa_1885).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horatio Seymour Squyer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The network was primarily the work of free and enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>, assisted by abolitionists and other sympathisers. Conductors included free-born blacks, white abolitionists, the formerly enslaved, whether escaped or manumitted, and Native Americans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a>, who had escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, was one of the most famous and successful conductors. William Still, sometimes called the Father of the Underground Railroad, helped hundreds of people escape, as many as 60 a month, sometimes hiding them in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> home; he kept careful records and published them in 1872. Levi Coffin was known for aiding over 2,000 fugitives, and Charles Turner Torrey and his colleagues often transported 15 or 20 people at a time.</p><p>Thomas Downing, a free Black man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, hid freedom seekers in the basement of his oyster restaurant. Black mariners, free and enslaved, gave rides, information on routes and the names of trusted people. In 1855 Mary Meachum, a free Black woman, tried to help eight or nine people cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>; word reached police agents and slave catchers who waited on the far shore, and the attempt failed. On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, Silvia Hector Webber and her husband John Ferdinand Webber ferried people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and most of the help in the south came from Mexican labourers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a> and Nanticoke are among the Native American nations said to have sheltered freedom seekers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Estimates of the numbers vary widely. One estimate suggests that by 1850 approximately 100,000 slaves had escaped to freedom via the network, and the professor of Pan-African studies J. Blaine Hudson put the total at 500,000 or more by the end of the Civil War. An estimated 30,000 to 40,000 settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, half of them between 1850 and 1860; more than 30,000 were said to have escaped there in the network's 20-year peak period, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> figures account for only 6,000.</p><p>Many arrivals were disappointed. Life in Canada was difficult, racial discrimination was widespread, and newcomers competed with mass European immigration for jobs. In 1785 the city of Saint John, New Brunswick, amended its charter to bar Blacks from practising a trade, selling goods, fishing in the harbour or becoming freemen, and the provisions stood until 1870. With the outbreak of the Civil War many Black refugees left Canada to enlist in the Union Army, and thousands returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a> after it ended.</p><p>Not every attempt succeeded. In 1842 a Mexican man and an enslaved Black woman fled Jackson County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, on two horses and were caught at the Lavaca River; she was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and her husband was immediately lynched. Free Black people were also kidnapped and sold into slavery, as Solomon Northup was, in what is sometimes called the Reverse Underground Railroad. Northerners, in Andrew Delbanco's words, "began to realize slavery wasn't just a Southern issue" after 1850, and the perception that Northern states ignored the fugitive slave laws was a major justification offered for secession.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass.jpg" alt="Frederick Douglass" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_Douglass_(circa_1879)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Kendall Warren, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The place of white abolitionists in the story is disputed. Cheryl Janifer Laroche argued that Blacks, enslaved and free, were the main actors in the Underground Railroad, and Diane Miller wrote that historians have overlooked African American agency by portraying it as an effort of white religious groups. Larry Gara argued that many of its stories belong to folklore, and that exaggerating figures such as Tubman, Thomas Garrett and Levi Coffin minimises the agency of enslaved Black people who liberated themselves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> objected in 1845 to the "very public manner" in which some western friends conducted what they called the Underground Railroad, which he thought made it "the upperground railroad" and put slave owners on their guard.</p><p>Maroon communities, such as those in the Great Dismal Swamp, were another route; Herbert Aptheker found evidence of fifty between 1672 and 1864, and the historical archaeologist Dan Sayer argues that historians downplay them. The claim that quilt patterns signalled routes dates from the 1980s and is disputed: quilt historians and Giles Wright found no contemporary evidence, and scholars doubt that spirituals carried coded directions.</p><p>In 1998 Congress passed the National Underground Railroad Network to Freedom Act, and the National Park Service designates associated sites. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman">Harriet Tubman</a> Underground Railroad National Historical Park was created in 2013 and the Harriet Tubman National Historical Park in Auburn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, in 2017. September is observed as International Underground Railroad Month.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Underground Railroad was a network of secret routes and safe houses, organised from the 1780s and run chiefly by free and enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>, that helped people escape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to free states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Estimates of the numbers range from 100,000 by 1850 to 500,000 or more by 1865.</em></p>

